  "response": "## The hotspot picture\n\nConnectivity findings (`high_fan_in_fan_out`), with the repo's own percentile cutoffs — fan-in p95 = 9 / p99 = 10, fan-out p95 = 6 / p99 = 11:\n\n| File | fan-in | fan-out | direct / transitive importers | Also flagged |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** | 1 | 18 / 29 | large_file 424L, swallowed error, 7× sync I/O |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** | 4 / 7 | large_file 459L, 6 God Functions |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | **11** | 1 / 7 | `runEsbuild` 237L, 38-prop option bag |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | 10 | 3 | 10 / 29 | `buildFinished` 103L |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 / 30 | 2 unprefixed booleans |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 / 9 | unbounded `Promise.all` |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | 0 | 7 | 0 / 0 | `getRollupConfig` 122L, False Identity |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 | 1 / 7 | swallowed error, 2× `Date.now` |\n\nOne counting caveat before the order: several `large_function` findings are **nested inside each other**, not separate work. `src/index.ts` `build` (167–459) contains `<anonymous>` (183–456) contains `mainTasks` (260–453) — three findings, one body. Same for `postcss.ts` `postcssPlugin` (6–145) ⊃ `setup` (18–143), and `svelte.ts` `sveltePlugin` (9–124) ⊃ `setup` (17–122) ⊃ `<anonymous>` (31–121). Treat each nest as one item.\n\n## Triage order\n\n**1. `src/utils.ts` — split it.**\nFan-in 18 against a p99 cutoff of 10: nearly double the repo's 99th percentile, and the highest `blast_radius` in the scan (0.46, 29 transitive importers). It's also 424 lines (1.4× threshold) and demonstrably mixed-concern — the sync-I/O finding alone spans `removeFiles` (L79), `ensureTempDeclarationDir` (L173), and more at L249–250, while `jsoncParse` (L135) is a JSON parser in the same module. 18 importers each pulling a different slice is the definition of a kitchen sink. Splitting by responsibility (fs helpers / jsonc / path+string) converts one 18-fan-in node into several 3–6 fan-in nodes — the single largest blast-radius reduction available. It's also the *safest* load-bearing file to touch: `test_gap` is 0, so tests exist. Do the split as a pure move with re-exports from `utils.ts` first, then migrate importers, so the 18-file diff never lands atomically.\n\n**2. `src/index.ts` — extract, don't split the boundary.**\nFan-out 17 against a p99 cutoff of 11 — the highest in the repo — but fan-in is only 4. That asymmetry is the whole argument: the *outward* risk is small (4 direct importers), so this is a comprehension fix, not a ripple fix. The payoff is that one extraction pass resolves the 459-line `large_file`, the fan-out flag, and the biggest God Function nest at once — `build` at 293 lines is 4.9× the 60-line domain threshold, with `mainTasks` (194L) and `startWatcher` (79L) and `normalizeOptions` (96L) all sitting inside or beside it as ready-made module boundaries. `test_gap` 0 again. Also clears the \"False Identity\" on `build`/`buildAll` (a `build*` name calling `removeFiles`).\n\n**3. `src/esbuild/index.ts` — extract `runEsbuild`.**\nFan-out 11 sits exactly at the p99 cutoff, but fan-in is **1**. A single caller means the refactor is contained — you can restructure aggressively with one import site to fix. `runEsbuild` is 237 lines (4.0× threshold) and reads 38 distinct properties off one `options` bag, which is the specific shape that makes agent edits go wrong. High value, near-zero ripple. This can run in parallel with #2; the two files barely overlap.\n\n**4. `src/plugin.ts::buildFinished` — fix the function, leave the module.**\n103 lines, and the file carries `blast_radius` 0.45 across 10 direct importers. Extract in place. Do **not** restructure the module — see below.\n\n**5. `src/cli-main.ts::main`.** 153 lines, high severity, but `blast_radius` 0.15 with 2 importers. Real, low-leverage. Bottom of the list.\n\n## Leave alone\n\n**`src/plugin.ts` as a module.** Fan-in 10 is above the p99 cutoff, but the finding's own evidence says *\"9 of 10 importers take types only — a shared type module's coupling is compile-time, and being depended on is its job.\"* Splitting it trades zero runtime decoupling for churn across 10 files. The flag is correct that it's load-bearing and incorrect that it needs splitting.\n\n**`src/log.ts`.** Fan-in 9 is *exactly* the p95 cutoff and below p99 (10), with fan-out 0 — a logger that 9 modules import and that imports nothing is a correctly-shaped leaf. 4 of its 9 importers are type-only. The only worthwhile finding is renaming `onlyImportant`/`silent` (effort: quick).\n\n**`src/rollup.ts`.** Flagged at fan-out 7, but `blast_radius` 0 with **0 importers, 0 transitive** — nothing in the repo imports it. `getRollupConfig` is genuinely bad (122 lines, and a `get*` name that calls `setSilent` and `removeFiles`), but there is no blast radius to reduce. Take the rename only; it's a one-line fix with no ripple by construction.\n\n**`src/api-extractor.ts`.** Fan-out 6 is exactly at the p95 cutoff with fan-in 1 — a marginal flag. Worth taking are the cheap in-place fixes (swallowed error at L197, the two `Date.now()` at L183/185, `writeFileSync` at L119/165), not a restructure.\n\n**`schema.json`.** 362-line `large_file` with 0 importers. It's a declarative artifact; \"split by responsibility\" is the wrong shape for a JSON schema. Suppress it.\n\n**`test/utils.ts`.** Fan-in 9 with 9 direct importers is what a test helper is *for*, and its findings (unbounded `Promise.all` over `Object.keys(files)`, one `readFileSync`) are in test infrastructure where the collection size is authored, not runtime data.\n\n## Two caveats on this ranking\n\n`churn` and `recency` are **0 on every finding** in this scan — there's no git history signal here. The order above is purely structural. If churn data were available, a high-churn file with moderate fan-in would likely outrank a static one with fan-in 18, since blast radius only costs you when the file actually changes.\n\nAnd the repo has no `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` (`crime_00007`, medium, effort: small). Before an agent touches `utils.ts` with its 18 importers, that file is the cheapest risk reduction on the board.",
